 ## Overview
-Array Based Ray Casting is an object-order ray casting architecture developed at the University of New South Wales that utilizes two parallel pipelined arrays (Warp Array and Ray Array) to rotate the volume dataset and cast rays [S1]. It solves the problem of high-performance volume rendering on PC-class hardware by implementing fully pipelined, parallel slice-by-slice processing [S1]. It differs from its parent, VolumePro, by relying on a rigid 2D array of processing elements with local communication rather than a more flexible software-defined or hybrid pipeline, and it is distinguished from sibling architectures like EM-Cube by its specific use of coordinate swapping for conflict-free memory access and shearing for rotation [S1].
+Array Based Ray Casting is an object-order ray casting architecture developed at the University of New South Wales that utilizes two parallel pipelined arrays (Warp Array and Ray Array) to rotate the volume dataset and cast rays [S1]. It solves the problem of high-performance volume rendering on PC-class hardware by implementing fully pipelined, parallel slice-by-slice processing [S1]. It differs from its parent, VolumePro, by relying on a rigid 2D array of processing elements with strictly local communication rather than a flexible software-defined pipeline, and it is distinguished from sibling architectures like EM-Cube by its specific use of coordinate swapping for conflict-free memory access and shearing for rotation [S1].
 
